# text = The report, which runs to more than four hundred pages and includes appendices on everything from soil chemistry to the migratory habits of local birds, concludes that the proposed highway would increase traffic on surrounding roads, reduce air quality in at least three neighborhoods, fragment the habitat of two protected species, and cost considerably more than the original estimate, but that it would also shorten average commute times by roughly eight minutes, create several thousand jobs during construction, and, according to the authors, provide long-term economic benefits that might or might not outweigh those costs depending on assumptions about fuel prices, population growth, and the future of remote work.
